        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tAugust 23, 1969, sold by Antonio Bustamante to Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 2016, bequeathed to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] According to Griffin’s Notebook 4-8.\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Sliced conch shell pectorals are typically worn by Ehecatl-Quetzalcoatl, a Mexica wind supernatural; the Ñuù Savi equivalent, koo savi, also wore the sliced conch pectoral, as seen in sixteenth-century painted manuscripts. The conch, when used as a sonorous instrument, has the potential to convert wind and, by extension, breath into thunderous sound. An artist carved an orange spondylus shell—a more prized shell than conch—emulating the sliced conch form, with spiked edges and a central swirl in relief. The spondylus shell carving in the form of a serpent head once may have been attached to a full serpent body, possibly with brilliant green quetzal plumes, thus representing Quetzalcoatl, whose name means “feathered serpent.” The reported findspots of these ornaments reflect the widespread influence of Central American religion throughout Mesoamerica in the Postclassic period.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T3G-7_CLA_FA.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
